WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.467 --> 00:00:02.302 line:0%
♪ (MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:07.340 line:0%
When you're young,
it's-- it's really difficult

00:00:07.406 --> 00:00:09.909 line:0%
to articulate the feelings
that you have,

00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:12.045 line:0%
<i>-whether it's anger, or love,</i>
-(SCREAMS)

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:14.013 line:0%
<i> or just being okay.</i>

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.683 line:0%
The idea for the show
on a conceptual level

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:20.052 line:0%
and on a visual level
is how do we allow our camera

00:00:20.319 --> 00:00:22.521 line:0%
<i> to articulate it
 for the characters.</i>

00:00:22.855 --> 00:00:24.924 line:0%
♪ (MUSIC CONTINUES) ♪

00:00:25.324 --> 00:00:28.461 line:0%
We want a-- a show that reflects
all the agonies,

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:32.465 line:0%
the ecstasies that life can be
for a teenager in any moment,

00:00:32.565 --> 00:00:35.067 line:0%
<i>but specifically, this moment
 feels very fraught.</i>

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:37.970 line:0%
The world's coming to an end
and I haven't even

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:39.439 line:0%
graduated high school yet.

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:43.809 line:0%
For so long, we've seen this
kind of gritty, handheld way

00:00:43.876 --> 00:00:47.113 line:0%
of telling a story that's maybe
a difficult pill to swallow.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.248 line:0%
<i> We wanted to move away
 from that idea</i>

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:52.652 line:0%
<i> and move into a realm
that was more expressionistic.</i>

00:00:53.786 --> 00:00:56.989 line:0%
Sam wanted to-- to make a--
a show about teenagers

00:00:57.056 --> 00:00:59.826 line:0%
that looks like
how they imagine themselves.

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:01.728 line:0%
(CAMERA CLICKS)

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:05.632 line:0%
<i> But at the same time,</i>

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:07.667 line:0%
you have to feel these
characters are really close.

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:11.203 line:0%
<i> So it's not about just
 being cool or innovative</i>

00:01:11.303 --> 00:01:14.140 line:0%
in a way, but more like,
being really personal.

00:01:16.742 --> 00:01:19.179 line:0%
♪ (MUSIC SOFTENS) ♪

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.550 line:0%
If we gave ourselves more rules,
in terms of our color palette,

00:01:24.851 --> 00:01:29.088 line:0%
<i>our camera, our movement,
 it ultimately allows us</i>

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.891 line:0%
<i>to be expressive without it
 feeling like</i>

00:01:31.957 --> 00:01:33.393 line:0%
it's gone off the rails.

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:40.133 line:0%
It's essentially a deeply
experiential, altered state.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:42.201 line:0%
MARCELL RÉV:
<i>He wanted to create a setting</i>

00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:44.537 line:0%
that can be dreamy
but based in reality.

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:47.473 line:0%
SAM LEVINSON:<i> Marcell has this
very specific sensitivity</i>

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:50.643 line:0%
<i> in terms of his care
 for faces and people.</i>

00:01:50.709 --> 00:01:52.745 line:0%
We dolly in and we push into
a close-up.

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:56.082 line:0%
<i>It feels like you're actually
breathing with these characters.</i>

00:01:56.582 --> 00:01:58.484 line:0%
One of our challenges
that we were excited to meet

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:01.153 line:0%
<i> was what can we do
 to make it feel alive?</i>

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.624 line:0%
<i>How do you create a living,
breathing, pulsing world?</i>

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:08.027 line:0%
LEVINSON:<i> How do we weave
 all of our action</i>

00:02:08.094 --> 00:02:10.864 line:0%
<i> into a space,
and to accomplish it seamlessly?</i>

00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:16.870 line:0%
♪ (MUSIC INTENSIFIES) ♪

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:20.072 line:0%
Just the rhythm
of the show itself

00:02:20.172 --> 00:02:21.808 line:0%
is a percussive rhythm.

00:02:23.542 --> 00:02:25.644 line:0%
JULIO PEREZ:<i> These varied
rhythms and textures make</i>

00:02:25.711 --> 00:02:28.481 line:0%
any given experience
more interesting.

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:31.750 line:0%
<i> The rhythmic pattern
for someone who's dealing with</i>

00:02:31.817 --> 00:02:35.755 line:0%
a really bad bout of depression
is likely to be very different

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:38.324 line:0%
<i>than someone who's thrilled
 about</i>

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:39.792 line:0%
<i> a certain development
 in their lives.</i>

00:02:40.659 --> 00:02:42.495 line:0%
<i>I feel super lucky that I get
 to play</i>

00:02:42.561 --> 00:02:43.763 line:0%
<i>with these different modes.</i>

00:02:49.001 --> 00:02:52.638 line:0%
LEVINSON:<i> Because the narrative
of it is so freewheeling,</i>

00:02:52.738 --> 00:02:56.408 line:0%
<i>it allows it to-- to go wherever
her mind wants to take it.</i>

00:02:56.775 --> 00:02:59.178 line:0%
<i>That's sort of how the scripts
 and the stories unfold.</i>

00:02:59.478 --> 00:03:01.614 line:0%
How would Rue tell this story?

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:03.850 line:0%
♪ (MUSIC CONCLUDES) ♪

